  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Head Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The head is where many of the day's strains collect. Frontal and tension headaches, dizziness, eye strain from screens, sinus congestion, and the mental fatigue of long, demanding work all have acupressure targets on the scalp, forehead, and skull base. These points are often the first a practitioner reaches for, because they sit close to the tissues you can feel — the forehead muscles, the temples, and the hollow at the base of the skull where neck tension often rises upward. For broader coverage, our guides to acupressure for headaches and migraines show how head points combine with hand and neck points, while the sleep and anxiety guides pair them with points on the face and wrists.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">From a TCM view, the scalp carries several Yang meridians — the Governing Vessel up the midline and the Gallbladder and Bladder pathways along the sides — and points here help regulate the flow of qì toward the head. At the physical level, pressing the temples, forehead, and occipital ridge eases tight muscles and relaxes the nerves that run beneath them, which is why these points are commonly used when pressure-related head discomfort builds during the day.</p>
  </section>

