  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Head Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Settle into a quiet, upright position and use the pads of your index and middle fingers. Press at a 90-degree angle, increasing the pressure slowly over 10–15 seconds until you feel a deep, dull ache or heaviness (deqi). Breathe evenly and hold for 30–60 seconds, then release just as gradually. For the temples and skull-base hollows, locate the soft depression first — keep the pressure gentle on top of the head, and never press on the soft fontanelle area of an infant's scalp.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is it safe to press points on the head?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For most healthy adults, yes, when done gently. Use light, even pressure with your fingertips, target the soft depressions rather than bone, and stop if any spot feels painful rather than achingly full. Never press through broken or irritated skin. If you are on blood-thinning medication or have a bleeding disorder, check with your clinician first, and seek medical care for sudden, severe, or unrelenting head pain rather than self-treating.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can scalp points help with mental fatigue?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Scalp points such as GV20 at the crown are traditionally used to clear heaviness and refresh the mind, and Yintang between the eyebrows is often pressed during long study or work sessions. A slow 30–60 second press forces a pause, encourages even breathing, and brings circulation to the head. It is not a substitute for sleep or hydration, but many people find it a useful reset when concentration dips.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For the head, hold each point for 30–60 seconds, pressing both sides where the point exists on both sides of the head. Move slowly, increase pressure over 10–15 seconds, and keep breathing evenly. You are aiming for a deep, dull sensation rather than pain — if something feels sharp or tender, ease off. You can repeat a short session a few times a day when symptoms build.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

